Nanoparticles (NPs) have attracted immense attention due to their attractive optical/electronic properties and potential applications.[1] In recent years, extensive efforts have been devoted to the assembly of NPs into ordered structures such as chains and sheets in order to transfer their properties from nanoscale to macroscale.[1-2] Self- or directed assembly of NP superlattice in one-dimensional (1D),[3] two-dimensional (2D)[4] and three-dimensional (3D)[5] forms has been reported. Among various assemblies, NP rings are of particular interests.[6] From a molecular stand-point, NPs have been viewed as “atoms” to build “artificial” molecules.[7] While 1D NP asembly mimicks long chain polymers, a NP ring mimicks the intriguing cyclic polymer, which has demonstrated distinct molecular properties compared with linear chains.[8]
Despite extensive research in NP assembly and metallic ring fabrication, due to the lack of control over NP interactions, it remains a challenging task to synthesize NP ring with precise structural control.[9] A few templating methods have been reported on NP ring fabrication, including lithography,[10] solvent evaporation,[11] nano droplet template,[12] dewetting,[13] colloidal lithography,[14] breath figure,[15] magnetic dipolar interaction,[13, 16] etc. These approaches have their own drawbacks, which mainly arise from the difficulty in precisely controlling the ring size. Synthesizing well-controlled free-standing cyclic 1D NP chain structure has yet been accomplished.
Herein we report a method to assemble gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) into precisely controlled and free-standing AuNP frames using poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO) single crystals as the template. The frame structure in this work has a square shape, topologically resembling NP nanorings and 1D cyclic chains. Polymer single crystals (PSCs) in general are organic 2D materials with typical thickness of ${\sim}5-10~\mathrm{nm}$ and a typical aspect ratio of $\sim10->10,000$ (width/thickness). Programed
PSC growth has gained renewed interests recently as it provides a powerful means for directed polymer assembly.[17] Upon crystallization, when end-functionalized polymers are used, the functional groups can be excluded onto PSC surface to immobilize NPs.[18] Previous studies have demonstrated that AuNPs could be absorbed onto the surface of thiol-terminated PEO PSCs through coupling AuNPs and the thiol groups.[18a-c] This type of NP-PSC hybrids can find applications in asymmetric NP synthesis,[19] directed NP assembly,[18c, 18d] surface enhanced Raman spectroscopy,[20]  catalyst support,[21] and autonomous nanomotors.[22] However, one limitation of these unique hybrid structures is the lack of resistance to organic solvents because PSCs can be easily dissolved, and the structure is then dissembled. In this work, by using a block copolymer (BCP) comprising of a PSC-forming and a NP-binding block, stable, precisely controlled free-standing AuNP frames were synthesized. The size and width of this unique assembly were readily controlled, which in turn were used to tune the Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) property of the frames. We envisage that our work provides a new and generic approach to synthesize NP frame/cyclic NP assemblies that are otherwise difficult to achieve.
The synthetic strategy is illustrated in Scheme 1. PSC templates were fabricated through solution crystallization of dimethoxyterminated PEO (PEO-2OMe, $M_{\mathrm{n}}{=}~5\mathrm{K}~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mol^{-1}},$ by a self-seeding method.[23] Poly(ethylene oxide)- $\cdot b$ -poly(4-vinylpyridine) (PEO- $b$ - P4VP) $(5\mathrm{K}{-}5\mathrm{K}\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ) was synthesized via ATRP and used to form P4VP brush frames around the PEO PSC templates. P4VP was chosen because the pendant pyridine groups can be coupled with AuNPs via coordinate bonding. Furthermore, one P4VP segment can couple with multiple AuNPs and one AuNP can bind with multiple P4VP segments, leading to effective crosslinking among P4VP and AuNPs. PEO-2OMe PSCs (shown in Scheme 1b) were obtained after crystallization at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . PEO- $b$ -P4VP 1-BuOH solution was then added to form P4VP brush frames around the PSCs (Scheme 1c). The P4VP brush frames were then incubated with $10\mathrm{nm}$ AuNPs solution to form AuNP frames (Scheme 1d). Excessive P4VP solution was subsequently added to further crosslink the AuNPs (Scheme 1e). After the dissolution of the PEO PSC, free-standing AuNP frames were formed (Scheme 1f, for clarity, PEO chains are not shown). The details of the synthesis and characterization of PEO- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{b}$ -P4VP and preparation of the AuNP frames can be found in Supporting Information (see Figure S1).
The successful crosslinking of the AuNPs can be confirmed by TEM and AFM. Figure 1d shows the TEM image of the free AuNP frame after completely dissolving the PEO PSC template. It clearly indicates that the AuNPs still retain the frame structure with a size of 10 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (or a total circumference of $40\upmu\mathrm{m}$ considering shape distortion). The enlarged image of the corner of the frame (Figure 1f) shows that the width of the frame is still $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which indicates the complete and uniform crosslinking over the whole frame area. A high resolution TEM image of AuNPs in the frames can be found in Figure S3. Figure 1f also reveals that the randomly absorbed AuNPs on PSC template shown in Figure 1e disappeared after PSC dissolution, confirming the complete removal of the inner PSC template. The dissolution of the PSCs can also be confirmed by the AFM analysis in Figure 2d and j: the frame remains after the dissolution and there is no height difference between the inner and outer areas of the AuNP frame, indicating that the PSC was washed away.  Quantitative production and high yield of free AuNP frames after dissolution can be seen in Figure S2b.Figure 1g reveals the dark field optical microscopy images of the free AuNP frames. The bright color arises from the AuNPs. The AuNPs retain the frame/ring shape and resemble cyclic AuNP chain. Of interest is the distortion of the AuNP frames from the original square shape when the PSC was dissolve (also shown in the Supporting movie). Figure 1h illustrates the distorted shape of the AuNP frame upon dissolution. This can be attributed to the soft P4VP linkage in between the AuNPs and the thin frame width. Therefore, the AuNP frames largely resemble NP nanoring structure as well as cyclic polymeric chains.
Noted that since self-seeding was used to grow PEO and BCP PSCs, these AuNP frames are monodispersed in size and width $(10\pm$ $0.15\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and $100\pm2$ nm respectively based on the image measurement of $\sim50$ frames). This opens the gateway to directed assembly of NPs into cyclic architectures using PSCs. Compared with the reported methods previously discussed, one of the advantages of using PSC as a template to assemble AuNPs is that the size and the width of the final AuNP frames can be easily controlled. We describe the generic frame structure in Figure 1 as $x N P_{\delta}-S F_{l}^{w}$ , where $\mathbf{X}$ denotes the type of NP, SF stands for square frame, $\delta,l.$ and $w$ are the diameter of the NP, the size and width of the frame, respectively. Using the present PSC templating method, all these parameters can be controlled. In the present study, we focus on $10\mathrm{nm}$ AuNPs using PEO crystal as the template, and this series of assembles can be described as $A u N P_{10}-S F_{l}^{w}$ .
To demonstrate the precise control of the size and width of $A u N P_{10}-S F_{l}^{w}$ , a series of growth conditions have been explored.  The size of the PSC can be tuned by controlling the seeding temperature. The PSC size increases as the seeding temperature increases, leading to the increase of the AuNP frame size, and vice versa. Here, we demonstrate that by tuning the seeding temperature from $43^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $42.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $43.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the AuNP frame size changed from $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , respectively. TEM images of the ${5\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ free AuNP frames are shown in Figure 3a-b. The width of the AuNP frame is determined by the PEO- $b$ -P4VP frame width, which can be controlled by changing the relative amount of PEO- $\mathbf{\nabla}_{b}$ -P4VP added to the PEO-2OMe PSC ‘seeds’. In this work, for $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ PSC, 100, 200, and $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ wide frames were obtained as shown in Figure 3. In a similar way, ${5\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ frames were also prepared with a $100\mathrm{nm}$ width.
Therefore, precisely tuned $A u N P_{10}-S F_{10}^{0.5}$ , $A u N P_{10}-S F_{10}^{0.2}$ $A u N P_{10}-S F_{10}^{0.1}$ , $A u N P_{10}-S F_{5}^{0.1}$ , and $A u N P_{10}-S F_{20}^{0.1}$ have been successfully synthesized. Note that the unit in the above abbreviation for l and $w$ is $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Figure S4 shows the TEM images of these AuNP frames prior to dissolving the PSC templates. Regular square shape can be clearly seen with a clear AuNP frames surrounding each PSC. Figure 3i shows a dark field optical microscopy image of $A u N P_{10}-S F_{20}^{0.1}$ . Note that some of the frames are entangled, similar to polymer chains, while two frames appear to be broken, possibly due to mechanical field induced by flow.
Figure 4a shows the UV-Vis spectra of as-synthesized AuNPs and $A u N P_{10}-S F_{l}^{w}$ with different sizes and widths. Free AuNPs show a SPR band at $526\mathrm{nm}$ , which is a typical value for $10\mathrm{nm}$ AuNP.[3c, 25] The SPR bands of AuNP frames and square sheets are broadened and red-shifted by $20\textrm{-}40\ \mathrm{nm}$ due to the aggregation of AuNPs. Of interest is that shoulders are observed between $600{-}700~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in the UV-Vis spectra of AuNP frames and this cannot be ascribed to simple aggregation of AuNPs alone because in the AuNP sheet case, no shoulders in the SPR spectrum were observed. Detailed peak fitting (Supporting Information, Figure S5) reveals that the shoulders in the SPR spectra are from the secondary peaks between $600{-}700\mathrm{nm}$ and these secondary peaks can be attributed to the longitudinal SPR of AuNP chains as reported.[3a, 3c, 3e, 26] The weak intensity of the longitudinal peaks may be due to the relatively loose contact between adjacent AuNPs in the frames. More interestingly, it is observed that the longitudinal SPR bands of the AuNP frames red shift to high wavenumber as the aspect ratio of the frame side $(\mathsf{A R}\equiv l/w)$ increases (Figure 4b). This phenomenon suggests that optical properties of the AuNP frames are similar to gold nanorods, which also show an increase in the longitudinal band wavelength as the aspect ratio increases.[26a, 27]
In summary, we have developed a facile and versatile method to assemble AuNPs into well defined, free standing cyclic frame structure $A u N P_{10}-S F_{l}^{w}$ with PEO single crystals as the template. PEO- $\mathbf{\nabla}_{b}$ -P4VP BCP was used to form precursor frame around the PEO PSC template, and AuNPs were then immobilized and simultaneously crosslinked onto the P4VP frame. The size $l$ and width $w$ of free AuNP frames were precisely controlled by tuning the crystallization conditions. UV-Vis spectra indicated weak longitudinal SPR bands, which red-shifts linearly with the aspect ratio of the frames, suggesting the AuNP frames share similar optical properties with Au nanorods. Topologically, these frame structures resemble NP nanorings and cyclic polymeric chains. We anticipate that the reported method may lead to a new approach to synthesize precisely assembled, free-standing cyclic NP chains and nanorings.
